WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.160
From hokey death scenes to badly written monologues,
and characters appearing that make no sense

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.250
in the context of the movie, the 2010s had
many cringe-worthy acting moments that will

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.840
go down in cinema infamy.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.320
These are just a few of the worst offenders
of the decade.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.840
The concluding chapter of Christopher Nolan's
gritty Dark Knight trilogy ended with a bunch

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.960
of explosive bangs thanks to Bane and his
associates, but also with a bit of a fizzle

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:29.550
featuring the super unfortunate in-movie death
of Marion Cotillard's Miranda Tate, aka Talia

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.360
al Ghul.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.520
After orchestrating a series of huge protests
with a rising death toll of innocent Gothamites,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.020
Talia al Ghul crashes her truck and with a
melodramatic gasp and sigh, falls back dead

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.840
but not before detonating her final bomb.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.220
"My father's work…is done…"

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.620
The moment is so incredibly hokey and laughable
that it would have been perfectly matched

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.879
in the campy Adam West-led Batman TV series,
but coming during Nolan's take on the franchise,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.120
it feels hugely out of place.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.420
We don't see a lot of Shakespearean villain-style
monologues these days, and it's for good reason.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.450
They don't tend to sound authentic outside
Shakespeare's context, even for a megalomaniacal

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.619
character, and instead come across as painful
exposition that ends up being funny rather

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.240
than menacing.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.500
For example, look at Eddie Redmayne's portrayal
of Balem Abrasax in the Wachowskis' space

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.970
opera Jupiter Ascending, which follows a young
Earth woman named Jupiter who learns that

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.850
she's actually interstellar royalty a revelation
that puts Balem's control of the universe

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:35.340
in jeopardy.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.200
Jupiter Ascending is filled with cringeworthy
moments, from Channing Tatum's eyeliner to

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:44.960
Kunis' overly earnest performance, but Redmayne
wins for one of the worst acted scenes of

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:45.740
the past decade.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.880
"I CREATE LIFE…and I destroy it."

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.939
In the years since, Redmayne himself has admitted
his work in Jupiter Ascending left a lot to

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.640
be desired.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:00.500
He even told GQ in an interview:

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.760
"I won a prize for it for the worst performance
of the year.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.740
So, yeah, it was a pretty bad performance
by all accounts."

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.400
For many Harry Potter fans, the fact that
Hermione Granger ended up with Ron Weasley

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.100
and not Harry was an unsatisfying way to end
the characters' story.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.830
Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows Part
1 did its part to muddy those waters by featuring

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.250
an intimate scene between Harry and Hermione
while Ron is off looking for Salazar Slytherin's

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.300
locket Horcrux.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:32.100
In order to make a despondent Hermione buck
up for a minute, Harry asks her to dance,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.610
and they hip and dip around awkwardly to Nick
Cave's haunting song "O Children."

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.720
The actors look incredibly uncomfortable not
just dancing with each other, but on their

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.820
own, and no amount of fancy camera work could
have helped them pull off the scene.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:49.100
If it weren't for Nick Cave's vocals and beautiful
lyrics, the moment would have been a complete

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.820
disaster even as it foreshadows Ron's eventual
jealous blow-up about Harry and Hermione's

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.060
possibly romantic feelings for each other.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:03.310
Quentin Tarantino has a long history of inserting
himself into his movies, and Django Unchained

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.700
is arguably one of his worst cameo moments.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.450
This antebellum revisionist history features
Jamie Foxx as Django, an escaped slave assisted

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:17.360
by dentist-turned-bounty hunter Dr. King Schultz
out to wreak bloody vengeance on slavers,

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.960
including Calvin Candie, who has also kidnapped
his love Hildi Von Shaft.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:26.540
As Django's rampage heats up, the movie cuts
to a flashback in which Tarantino appears,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.230
speaking in a dodgy Australian accent before
Django kills him that completely derails the

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.400
climax of the film.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:38.560
"A little dynamite for you black fellas to
play with!"

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:39.620
Yahoo!

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:44.440
News UK suggested that Tarantino sported an
Australian accent because he couldn't do a

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.740
passable Southern one, which begs the question
of why he was even included in the movie at

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.490
all other than his director-sized ego, that
is.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.810
Sometimes there's just nothing even the best
actors can do when the script veers into overwrought

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.980
territory, which is unfortunately the case
with Interstellar, Christopher Nolan's story

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.700
about the intersection of science, technology,
and family as well as wormholes and event

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.460
horizons, since Nolan can't make a movie without
a mind-bending twist or two.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.180
Sadly, the audience is well yanked out of
the complicated but well-crafted story when

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.180
Anne Hathaway's character Amelia Brand prattles
off a Hallmark greeting card of a monologue

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.920
that sounds considerably less than scientific.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.660
"Love is the one thing we're capable of perceiving
that transcends dimensions of time and space."

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.180
This line comes as Brand makes her case that
love is a quantifiable force in the universe

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:40.850
that can actually be used as a tool of measurement
a pretty ridiculous concept, even though in

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:42.940
the logic of the movie, she turns out to be
right.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.100
But just because the movie makes the concept
real doesn't mean that this speech introducing

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.880
it works, and even Hathaway's considerable
talent can't make the monologue sound anything

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.460
but forced.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.400
Thankfully the scene is only two minutes of
pure cheddar in an otherwise compelling sci-fi

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.880
drama, but it's also enough to stick out as
one of the worst acted scenes of the past

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:01.460
decade.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.000
"Three Americans are hailed as heroes for
foiling an attack on a high-speed train in

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.500
Europe."

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.680
It was a real-life event that made news around
the globe: Three American men traveling from

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.620
Amsterdam to Paris thwarted a would-be terrorist
mass shooter through their own quick thinking

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:18.960
and selfless bravery.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.060
Spencer Stone, Anthony Sadler, and Alek Skarlatos
wrestled the weapon from the gunman and saved

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.860
the life of one of his victims, American Mark
Moogalian, who was the first to identify the

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:30.360
terrorist.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.180
Later, French authorities found the shooter
had been armed with 300 rounds of bullets,

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.120
more than enough to have killed most of the
people on the train had he not been stopped.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.840
Clint Eastwood directed The 5:17 to Paris
and, in a surprising move, decided to cast

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.780
the four American survivors, including the
gunshot victim Moogalian, as themselves.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.440
Unfortunately, the experiment didn't exactly
pay off in this big-budget Hollywood recreation

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.060
of real events.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.600
These men are heroes, and did an incredible
service for their fellow passengers, but they're

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.220
not professional actors, and it's very clear
to audiences.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.760
"Look at the baby soda, Spencer!"

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.700
"Alek, shut the heck up."

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:12.420
The 2010s saw Nicolas Cage in a number of
dreadful movies, but Drive Angry stands out

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.240
as one of the worst.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:19.010
The actor delivers a furious but empty turn
as John Milton, a man who's been dead 10 years

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:24.280
and returns to Earth from Hell in order to
save his infant granddaughter from being sacrificed

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:24.820
to Satan.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.800
His one-liners fall so flat you'd think an
ACME truck rolled over them.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:30.960
"Nothing of this Earth can kill me!"

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.100
"I'm not of this Earth."

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.100
It's hard to pick a least favorite scene the
absurd gunfight sex scene?

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.560
Milton peeing fire?

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.340
Cage rebounded in later years with more critically
acclaimed fare like the gonzo Mandy, but for

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:51.080
a decent chunk of the decade, it seemed like
he may have permanently lost his way and Drive

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.180
Angry is a perfect example of why.

